> So, let me abstract a level further.
> Suppose that there are two stages of memory model interaction.
> 1. Within-conversation: The within-conversation object persists for the length of a conversation. It has a function that is called once per response needed from the AI. For this it is given the AI's last response and the user's response in reply to that. It then returns a chunk of text that can be embedded into the prompt to the AI. It also has a function that can translate the whole conversation to that point into an effective summary.
> 2. Between-conversations: This "lives" on the disk. It takes in everything that happened in a conversation and makes decisions about what should be stored.
> 
> Obviously, the between-conversations material is then provided to the within-conversation memory object.
> 
> In case it's useful, I am currently using both `whoosh` and `sqlalchemy` as databases for the AI. The whoosh database currently just has all conversations, which are indexed to keywords using summaries. The `sqlalchemy` database is currently used to record and retrieve various "feed items" from around the internet. Those systems may not reflect best practices, but it might be nice to stick to those libraries (or make use of that stored content) if it is useful.
