> Yes — that sounds like a **good and clean abstraction**.
> 
> In fact, I think it’s better than jumping immediately into “episodic/semantic/task memory” as separate top-level classes. Your split is architecturally very natural:
> 
> - **Within-conversation memory** = working memory / prompt assembly / ongoing summary
> - **Between-conversations memory** = persistence / consolidation / retrieval across sessions
> 
> That’s exactly the right separation.
> 
> ## My main reaction
> 
> I would endorse this design, with a few refinements in naming and responsibilities so it stays robust.
> 
> ---
> 
> # 1. Your two-stage model is sound
> 
> ## A. Within-conversation object
> This should be responsible for:
> 
> - maintaining the conversation-local state
> - deciding what persistent memory is relevant right now
> - producing a compact prompt-ready memory block
> - maintaining a rolling summary of the current conversation
> 
> Your API idea is reasonable:
> - update per turn
> - emit prompt text
> - emit conversation summary
> 
> That’s good.
> 
> ## B. Between-conversations object
> Also good:
> - takes a finished conversation
> - decides what to store
> - updates durable memory
> - supports retrieval for future conversations
> 
> Yes, this is the right place for:
> - extracting candidate facts/preferences
> - storing episodic summaries
> - deduplicating
> - updating user/project/task models
> 
> ---
> 
> # 2. One important refinement: retrieval belongs partly in both layers
> 
> You wrote:
> 
> > Obviously, the between-conversations material is then provided to the within-conversation memory object.
> 
> Yes — but I would make that explicit in the API.
> 
> The persistent layer should not just “store”; it should also **retrieve** relevant prior memory for a new conversation or a new turn.
> 
> So conceptually:
> 
> - **Between-conversations memory**
>   - persistent storage
>   - indexing
>   - retrieval
>   - consolidation after conversation end
> 
> - **Within-conversation memory**
>   - turn-by-turn state
>   - current summary
>   - prompt assembly using retrieved durable memory
> 
> So the within-conversation object probably gets initialized with retrieved long-term memory, and may also ask for more during the conversation.
> 
