> # Optional: use actual pytest fixtures instead of builder functions
> 
> Right now these tests use:
> 
> ```python
> system = build_inmemory_test_system()
> ```
> 
> That is perfectly fine.
> 
> If you want more idiomatic pytest style, add a `tests/conftest.py` like this:
> 
> ```python
> import pytest
> 
> from memory.fixtures import build_inmemory_test_system
> 
> 
> @pytest.fixture
> def test_system():
>     return build_inmemory_test_system()
> 
> 
> @pytest.fixture
> def long_term_memory(test_system):
>     return test_system.long_term_memory
> ```
> 
> Then tests can become:
> 
> ```python
> def test_something(long_term_memory):
>     ...
> ```
> 
> But I would honestly start with the explicit builder calls first, because they are simpler and clearer.
> 
> ---
> 
> # One likely issue to watch for
> 
> Because `long_term.py` imports `ConversationMemory`, and `conversation.py` type-checks against `LongTermMemory`, you may be fine as written, but circular imports can sometimes bite.
> 
> If they do, the fix is usually:
> - keep runtime imports minimal
> - use `TYPE_CHECKING` for type-only imports
> - possibly move the `ConversationMemory` import inside `start_conversation()`
> 
> If you want, I can help harden that next if needed.
